{
  "case_id": "2022-22-148",
  "document_hash": "45a1d9a2e608ac47828291ce48d6a830256f15642804891e3f6a9157452eb597",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Supreme Court",
      "case_id": "2022-22-148",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "45a1d9a2e608ac47828291ce48d6a830256f15642804891e3f6a9157452eb597",
      "evidence_id": "ev-45a1d9a2e608-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Argued March 22, 2023\u2014Decided June 8, 2023"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-22-148",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "45a1d9a2e608ac47828291ce48d6a830256f15642804891e3f6a9157452eb597",
      "evidence_id": "ev-45a1d9a2e608-0000-0007",
      "kind": "holding",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We hold only that it is not appropriate when the accused infringer has used a trademark to designate the source of its own goods\u2014in other words, has used a trademark as a trademark."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-22-148",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "45a1d9a2e608ac47828291ce48d6a830256f15642804891e3f6a9157452eb597",
      "evidence_id": "ev-45a1d9a2e608-0000-0008",
      "kind": "holding",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The use of a mark does not count as noncommercial just because it parodies, or otherwise comments on, another's products."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Jack Daniel's",
      "case_id": "2022-22-148",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "45a1d9a2e608ac47828291ce48d6a830256f15642804891e3f6a9157452eb597",
      "evidence_id": "ev-45a1d9a2e608-0000-0012",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Soon after Bad Spaniels hit the market, Jack Daniel's sent VIP a letter demanding that it stop selling the product."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2022-22-148",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "45a1d9a2e608ac47828291ce48d6a830256f15642804891e3f6a9157452eb597",
      "evidence_id": "ev-45a1d9a2e608-0001-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 13,
        "start": 13
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The District Court rejected both contentions for a common reason: because VIP had used the cribbed Jack Daniel's features as trademarks\u2014that is, to identify the source of its own products."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2022-22-148",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "45a1d9a2e608ac47828291ce48d6a830256f15642804891e3f6a9157452eb597",
      "evidence_id": "ev-45a1d9a2e608-0001-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 13,
        "start": 13
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case thus proceeded to a bench trial, where Jack Daniel's prevailed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-22-148",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "45a1d9a2e608ac47828291ce48d6a830256f15642804891e3f6a9157452eb597",
      "evidence_id": "ev-45a1d9a2e608-0001-0003",
      "kind": "holding",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 16,
        "start": 15
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Without deciding whether Rogers has merit in other contexts, we hold that it does not when an alleged infringer uses a trademark in the way the Lanham Act most cares about: as a designation of source for the infringer's own goods."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2022-22-148",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "45a1d9a2e608ac47828291ce48d6a830256f15642804891e3f6a9157452eb597",
      "evidence_id": "ev-45a1d9a2e608-0002-0000",
      "kind": "holding",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "However wide the scope of the \u201cnoncommercial use\u201d exclusion, it cannot include, as the Ninth Circuit thought, every parody or humorous commentary."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2022-22-148",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "45a1d9a2e608ac47828291ce48d6a830256f15642804891e3f6a9157452eb597",
      "evidence_id": "ev-45a1d9a2e608-0002-0002",
      "kind": "concurrence",
      "opinion_part": "concurrence",
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Survey answers may refect a mistaken belief among some survey respondents that all parodies require permission from the owner of the parodied mark."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2022-22-148",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "45a1d9a2e608ac47828291ce48d6a830256f15642804891e3f6a9157452eb597",
      "evidence_id": "ev-45a1d9a2e608-0002-0003",
      "kind": "concurrence",
      "opinion_part": "concurrence",
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I am pleased to join the Court's opinion. I write separately only to underscore that lower courts should handle Rogers v. Grimaldi, 875 F. 2d 994 (CA2 1989), with care."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
